{"id":"902c07ae-c876-4c82-93da-b98829921402","object":"chat.completion","created":1785004809,"model":"deepseek-v4-flash","choices":[{"index":0,"message":{"role":"assistant","content":"{\"implementation\":\"def solve(case):\\n    tokens = case['tokens']\\n    calls = case['calls']\\n    # Sort candidates: descending score, then ascending start, then ascending call_id\\n    sorted_calls = sorted(calls, key=lambda c: (-c['score'], c['start'], c['call_id']))\\n    selected = []\\n    for c in sorted_calls:\\n        overlap = False\\n        for s in selected:\\n            # half-open intervals: [start, end)\\n            if c['start'] < s['end'] and s['start'] < c['end']:\\n                overlap = True\\n                break\\n        if not overlap:\\n            selected.append(c)\\n    # Order selected by start (ascending) for reporting\\n    selected.sort(key=lambda c: c['start'])\\n    selected_call_ids = [c['call_id'] for c in selected]\\n    # Apply replacements from right to left\\n    tokens_out = list(tokens)\\n    for c in reversed(selected):\\n        tokens_out[c['start']:c['end']] = c['replacement_tokens']\\n    return {'selected_call_ids': selected_call_ids, 'tokens': tokens_out}\"}","reasoning_content":"We need to implement solve(case) that takes a case payload with tokens and calls. The task is to apply accepted API calls to text while preserving registered span order, rejecting overlaps, keeping all eligible calls? Actually \"all_eligible_calls\" property suggests we select calls based on some criteria. The description: \"Rank candidates by descending score, start position, then call ID. Greedily reject any span overlapping an already selected span. Order accepted calls by source start for reporting. Apply replacements from right to left so earlier offsets do not shift. Return selected IDs and the exact interleaved token stream.\"\n\nSo we have a list of calls, each with start, end (indices into tokens list, presumably half-open? The start is inclusive, end exclusive? The examples: start 1, end 3 covers tokens t1,t2? That matches typical. Replacement tokens are a list of strings to substitute for that span. We need to select a subset of calls that do not overlap (based on start and end indices) and order them by start ascending. Then we apply replacements from right to left (i.e., process calls in descending order of start index) to avoid offset shifts. Then output selected_call_ids (list of call_ids in order of start) and tokens (the final interleaved token stream after replacements).\n\nBut there is also ranking: \"Rank candidates by descending score, start position, then call ID.\" That means we sort candidates (all calls) by descending score, then ascending start, then ascending call ID? Actually \"descending score, start position, then call ID\" – start position is ascending? Usually tie-breaking: if scores equal, smaller start first? The phrase \"start position\" without direction could be ascending. Then call ID ascending. Then we greedily select: iterate through sorted list, if the current call's span does not overlap with any already selected, add it. That ensures we pick highest scoring non-overlapping calls. This yields a set of accepted calls.\n\nThen order accepted calls by source start for reporting (ascending start). Then apply replacements from right to left (descending start) so earlier offsets are not affected. Then construct final token list: start with original tokens, then for each selected call in descending start order, replace the slice tokens[start:end] with the replacement_tokens list. Because we go rightmost first, indices remain valid. After all replacements, we have final tokens.\n\nReturn selected_call_ids (ordered by start) and final tokens.\n\nImportant: The output schema requires selected_call_ids as array of strings, tokens as array of strings. No other keys.\n\nAlso note that the implementation must be pure Python, no external libs. Use only standard library.\n\nThe fixture shows calls with replacement_tokens lists. Could be multiple tokens.\n\nEdge cases: overlapping spans after greedy selection? We reject overlaps. But what about calls that are exactly same start/end? They overlap. Only one can be selected.\n\nWe need to ensure that we handle ties correctly: sorting by (-score, start, call_id) or (key=lambda x: (-x['score'], x['start'], x['call_id'])). But note that call_id is a string; lexicographic order is fine.\n\nAlso note that the payload may have other keys like \"_case_nonce\". We ignore.\n\nNow write solve(case) function. It takes a dict (payload) with keys 'tokens' and 'calls'. We'll implement.\n\nImplementation steps:\n1. Extract tokens list, calls list.\n2. Sort calls by key: (-score, start, call_id). Use lambda.\n3. Initialize selected list empty.\n4. For each call in sorted order:\n   - Check if its span [start, end) overlaps any already selected call's span. Overlap if max(start, selected_start) < min(end, selected_end). Because intervals are half-open: start inclusive, end exclusive. Overlap condition: start < selected_end and selected_start < end.\n   - If no overlap, add to selected.\n5. Sort selected by start ascending (and tie-break? Not needed but could by call_id).\n6. Extract selected_call_ids in that order.\n7. To apply replacements: make a copy of tokens? We need to build new list. Since we modify from rightmost, we can work on a list copy. For each call in selected sorted by start descending (i.e., reverse sorted by start), do: replacement = call['replacement_tokens']; tokens_list[call['start']:call['end']] = replacement. That is list slice assignment.\n8. Return {\"selected_call_ids\": selected_call_ids, \"tokens\": final_tokens}\n\nMake sure to handle cases where tokens is empty, calls empty, etc.\n\nNow we need to output as JSON with key \"implementation\" containing the Python code as a string. Escape newlines, backslashes, quotes.\n\nI'll write the code as a string, then embed in JSON.\n\nLet's produce